[size=5]Description[/size]

A load-plan overlay for Star Citizen haulers: it decides which destination goes into which rack before you load, and shows it in a translucent window next to the game.

How it works: the [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/wiki]wiki[/url]. Source code: [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan]GitHub[/url].

[b]What it reads[/b]
[list]
[*]When you press the scan key (F3, or the editor's Scan button), it copies the contract area of the Star Citizen window and reads it with its own OCR on your PC. Nothing is sent anywhere, and it reads no other program's data.
[/list]

[b]Network access[/b]
The app goes online for two things only, both to github.com, both plain downloads; nothing about you or your PC is sent.
[list]
[*][b]Ship holograms (off until you pick one):[/b] ship holograms are made from CIG's ship models and are not under MIT. They live in a release of their own, holo-v3, with its own NOTICE, not in this ZIP. The app downloads them only from there, one file per ship when you pick a hologram look, and never fetches anything from Fleetyards; [b]Download ship holograms[/b] in the settings switches it off. They derive from game models and may be withdrawn on request.
[*][b]Update check (on until you turn it off):[/b] once per start, after the overlay is up, the app asks GitHub's release list of this project whether a newer version is out, and offers it at the bottom of the overlay. It downloads only when you click [b]Update[/b]. Turn off [b]Check for updates on startup[/b] in the settings, section [b]Updates[/b], and it makes no request at all.
[*][b]Updates come from GitHub, not from Nexus.[/b] The file here is the same ZIP as the GitHub release; once it runs, the app updates itself from the GitHub releases.
[/list]

[b]Known issues[/b]
[list]
[*][b]Unsigned:[/b] Windows warns the first time you start a downloaded ZIP.
[*][b]A folder below Program Files:[/b] an update asks Windows for administrator rights; say No and the old version comes back with [b]Update failed · cancelled[/b]. Extract the ZIP to a folder you can write to.
[*][b]Ship data:[/b] the racks of 102 ships come from the game files, each placed to the nearest whole cell; the 135c, 600i Executive Edition and F7C Hornet Mk II are still drafts from Fleetyards. The game files describe no doors, so some racks may have a door limit the app doesn't know.
[*][b]The Retaliator is not included.[/b]
[*][b]A crate under an empty rack[/b] in front of it can't be clicked in the ship view. Turn the view, or use its line in the legend.
[*][b]Very large ships zoomed in all the way[/b] (such as the Hull C): the camera can stand among the racks, and parts close to it are cut off. Zoom out a little.
[/list]
More, with workarounds: [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/wiki/Troubleshooting#known-issues]Troubleshooting › Known issues[/url].

[b]Bugs and questions[/b]
Please report bugs on GitHub, not in the Nexus comments, so they can be tracked.
[list]
[*]Bugs, wrong cargo grids, ideas: [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/issues/new/choose]Issues[/url] · questions: [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/discussions]Discussions[/url]
[*]Copy the version from the footer of the settings window (e.g. [b]SC-CargoPlan 0.5.0+…[/b]).
[*]Open a [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/issues/new?template=bug.yml]bug report[/url] (needs a GitHub account) and fill in the form.
[/list]

[size=5]Installation instructions[/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download [b]SC-CargoPlan-v<version>-win-x64.zip[/b] from the Files tab, or from the [url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/releases/latest]latest release[/url] on GitHub.
[*]Right-click it → [b]Extract All[/b] into a [b]new[/b] folder you can write to (not Program Files). Don't extract it over an older version: the ZIP has no folder of its own any more.
[*]Start [b]SC-CargoPlan.exe[/b] in that folder. Windows warns that the app is unsigned: [b]More info → Run anyway[/b].
[*]Coming from v0.4.0 or older: your settings and plan stay (they live in [b]%LocalAppData%\SC-CargoPlan[/b]); delete the old folder afterwards.
[/list]
From then on the app updates itself from GitHub (see Network access).
Check the download against the [b].sha256[/b] file of the GitHub release ([url=https://github.com/MRiediger/sc-cargoplan/wiki/Installation-and-first-start#check-the-download-optional]how[/url]).

Run Star Citizen in [b]Borderless[/b] window mode; exclusive fullscreen covers the overlay.

[size=5]Main features[/size]

[list]
[*]One color per destination, one rack per destination where the ship allows it, every crate named in the legend.
[*]Optionally a block per destination in a shared rack, with a gap to get at the crates.
[*]Press F3 on an open contract: CargoPlan reads it itself and adds it to the plan; or type it in the editor.
[*]Drag, turn, split and merge crates to match what the cargo elevator really did.
[*]Mark each delivery loaded, then delivered at its stop; undo any step.
[*]Click-through while the game has the mouse. A separate window: nothing is injected into the game.
[*]105 ships. Racks, hull and outline of 102 of them come from the game files; three still use Fleetyards data.
[/list]

[size=5]Requirements[/size]

[list]
[*]Windows 10/11 x64 (tested on Windows 11). Nothing else to install: .NET comes with it.
[/list]

[size=5]Shout outs[/size]

[list]
[*]Cargo grid data and ship models from [url=https://cryon.rocks/ops/cargo]cryon.rocks (Motherlode)[/url], extracted from the Star Citizen game files and used with its operators' permission: the racks, the hull holograms and the outlines come from the same game data.
[*]Three ships (135c, 600i Executive Edition, F7C Hornet Mk II) still use hull data from [url=https://fleetyards.net]fleetyards.net[/url]: their grids, hologram and outline. Fleetyards' data made the first ship list possible.
[*]Thanks to both for letting this project use their data.
[*]Stop colors: the [url=https://jfly.uni-koeln.de/color/]Okabe-Ito palette[/url] and [url=https://sronpersonalpages.nl/~pault/]Paul Tol's[/url] schemes.
[*]Not affiliated with Motherlode, Fleetyards or CIG. Star Citizen is a trademark of Cloud Imperium Rights LLC.
[/list]
Designed and tested in the game by the author; code and texts written with an AI coding assistant.

MIT licensed. Not under MIT: the ship silhouettes ([b]outlines/NOTICE.md[/b]) and the ship holograms (NOTICE of the holo-v3 release).
